  "live_records": {
    "q-36502": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36502",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 10-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
