    "q-82718": {
      "dom_id": "step-q82718",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
