      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
